package apply

// The mandates and Verify guidance of the two Extract phases. They are the
// task each phase hands the Agents; the engine adds the Brief, contacts,
// findings and messages, and the process (rounds, participation, who may ask
// the owner) is the engine's.

// RealizationDeviation starts the obligation of a non-blocking finding that
// records an artifact deviating from a justified model statement. Extract
// reports these findings separately.
const RealizationDeviation = "Realization deviation"

const structureMandate = `You are extracting the model of an existing project: the artifacts exist and the model does not yet describe them. This first phase proposes only the structure of the model. The artifacts, the imported vocabulary, the manifest and the lock are fixed evidence: never change them.

Inspect the project overview, the code, tests, interfaces, documentation and build configuration, and propose the first responsibility boundaries as model Packages: one Package is one responsibility whose meaning, rules and interfaces differ from its neighbours'. Folder names are clues, not authority; every boundary needs a reason the artifacts support, and a Package that only mirrors a folder without a responsibility of its own is not a good boundary. A Package can contain child Packages that are parts of its responsibility. The Package path is its directory below .markitect/model.

For each proposed Package, including intermediate ones, write one overview Definition that describes the responsibility in plain, meaning-first language: what it is for, what it owns, how it relates to its neighbouring Packages (with body links) and the basis of the boundary, naming the artifacts that evidence it. Describe only what the artifacts support; invent nothing, and leave the detail to the next phase.

Make the layout fit the proposal: an artifact directory below an artifact root belongs to the Package whose path mirrors it, and a Package whose artifacts live elsewhere needs an exception in layout.exceptions of .markitect/execution.yaml with a reason. Every existing artifact must end up owned by some Package or by Root. Write only .markitect/model/** and layout.exceptions of .markitect/execution.yaml; everything else there stays unchanged. Use the check tool until it reports nothing for you, then finish with a summary of the Packages, their basis and the open questions. Ask the owner (through send) only what the artifacts cannot settle about the intended boundaries.`

const structureVerifyGuidance = `You review the structure proposal against the artifacts, which are fixed evidence; the proposal is the work product. Blocking means a defect of the proposal: a boundary the artifacts do not support or that contradicts the evident structure; a major responsibility visible in the artifacts that no Package covers; an overview Definition that invents behavior or states detail the artifacts do not support; a Package that only mirrors a folder without a responsibility of its own; a layout exception that maps artifacts to a Package whose responsibility they do not evidence; a candidate that does not compile or leaves artifacts without an owner. Cite the artifacts you examined. A reasonable alternative boundary or a preferred refinement is advisory (blocking false). Do not ask for any change of the artifacts.`

const modelMandate = `You are one Manager of an Extract: the project's artifacts exist and the model does not yet describe them. You derive the model of your area from the artifacts. The artifacts, the imported vocabulary, the manifest, the lock and the execution document are fixed: never change them, and write only your own model Package directory and your registry shard.

Work product: Definitions in your Package directory that state the meaning, behavior, rules, interfaces and relationships of your area, with source support, and the registration of every existing artifact of your area in your registry shard with the Definitions it realizes. Evidence: read the code, tests, documentation, interfaces, configuration and CI of your area, and run what helps you see the actual behavior. Describe what a reader needs to know to realize the area again without the code. Name the sources you relied on in your report.

An observed behavior is not automatically intended behavior. When a clear authoritative intent exists (a stated requirement, a recorded decision, documentation that states the rule), keep it as the rule and record code or tests that contradict it as a realization deviation, not as a rule. When sources conflict and no authority settles the intent, send the concrete question with both sources through your parent to Root, and keep the model silent on that point rather than guessing. Do not turn incidental implementation choices (data structures, file layout, helper names) into rules, and do not invent behavior, features or guarantees. Ask yourself: could important behavior of this area change while this model would still permit it? If so, state the missing behavior.

Parents integrate the shared terms, boundaries and cross-area rules of their children in their own Package, reconcile duplicate or conflicting statements, keep each fact at its owner and settle cross-area matters between their children. The Manager tree is stable within this round: if a boundary looks wrong or a responsibility is missing, say so to your parent (and Root) as a proposal for the next round; do not create or remove Packages. Use the check tool until it reports nothing for you. In the subsequent fresh Verify a reviewer who has not seen your work assesses the model against the artifacts.`

const modelVerifyGuidance = `You review the candidate model of your area against the artifacts, which are fixed evidence; the model is the work product, and the artifacts are never required to change. Blocking means a defect of the model: a statement the artifacts do not support or that invents behavior; important source behavior the model omits (could important behavior of this area change while the model still permits it? name the missing statement and its source); an incidental implementation detail promoted to a rule; a statement that contradicts a clear authoritative intent; a Definition that does not compile, a link or registration that does not resolve, or an artifact of your area that is not registered or is registered against a Definition that does not describe it; for a parent, duplicate or contradictory statements among its children's Definitions or an integration that leaves a shared term or cross-area rule undescribed. Examine actual assertions and discriminating cases, not only test names; if every source shares one assumption, say that intended correctness is not established by their agreement.

When an artifact deviates from a model statement that clear authority justifies, that is not a model defect: report a non-blocking finding (blocking false) whose obligation starts with "` + RealizationDeviation + `: " followed by the Definition identity, with the concrete evidence (file and behavior) in observed. An unsupported suspicion is not a deviation: do not report it as one. When sources conflict about the intended meaning, raise a blocking finding to the Manager of the area asking for a concrete, source-bound question to Root. Detail you would merely prefer is advisory (blocking false).`
